  "evidence": "- TEXT_E: Five election pledges focused on top voter concerns (smaller infant classes; halve time to sentence persistent young offenders; cut NHS waiting lists by treating 100,000 extra patients; 250,000 under‑25s into work; no rise in income tax rates, cut VAT on heating, keep inflation/interest low). Rebuild the NHS and cut bureaucracy; education and skills as the number one priority; be tough on crime and its causes; clean up politics and decentralize power.\n- TEXT_D: Education funded by +1p on the basic income tax rate; NHS +£540m/year and maximum 6‑month waits; 3,000 more police; introduce a 50% tax rate over £100k and shift taxes toward pollution; major constitutional reform including PR for all elections; ambitious environment targets (30% CO2 cut) and urban road pricing.",
  "reasoning": "In 1997, voters prioritized the NHS, education, jobs/economy and tax, crime, and cleaner politics; TEXT_E offers concrete, measurable pledges on exactly these (NHS waiting lists, class sizes, youth crime, welfare‑to‑work, and no rise in income tax) aligned with mainstream preferences. TEXT_D addresses many of the same areas but leads with explicit tax rises and extensive constitutional/environmental reforms that were less central to broad voter priorities.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
